        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.666,
            0.848,
            0.741
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We can see that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta  AOB  \\cong \\Delta  COD"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta AOD \\cong \\Delta COB"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Hence, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{AB} = \\mathrm{CD}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{AD} = \\mathrm{CB},"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " since they are corresponding parts of congruent triangles. Therefore, ABCD is a rectangle since it satisfies the definition of a rectangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.782,
            0.685,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Will ABCD remain a rectangle if the angles between the diagonals are changed? Can we generalise this? Take one of the angles between the diagonals as x."
            }
